According to [p. 1], the message passing (MP) framework is based on the core idea of recursive neighborhood aggregation. It consists of an aggregation phase followed by a combination phase.

More specifically, in the MP framework, at each time step t+1, a message vector mt+1v is computed from the representations of the neighbors N(v) of v:

mt+1v = AGGREGATE t+1({
htw | w∈N(v)
})

Then, the new representation ht+1v of v is computed by combining its current feature vector ht v with the message vector mt+1v:

ht+1v = COMBINE t+1(
htv,mt+1v
)

This framework was formalized by (Gilmer et al. 2017) and (Xu et al. 2018a), and it is observed that many of the recently introduced Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) can be reformulated under this MP framework.
